1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,885
Why does coffee
leave a ring?

2
00:00:01,885 --> 00:00:03,290
Not… blobs.

3
00:00:03,887 --> 00:00:06,787
Spill a drop of coffee
and let it dry.

4
00:00:06,847 --> 00:00:09,817
It leaves a dark ring,
with a paler middle.

5
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:13,402
Coffee is mostly water,
with tiny brown specks

6
00:00:13,402 --> 00:00:14,462
in it.

7
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:16,192
Watch the specks.

8
00:00:16,257 --> 00:00:17,597
They don't stay put.

9
00:00:17,637 --> 00:00:19,832
They stream outward,
toward the edge.

10
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,880
The edge of the drop
is stuck to the paper.

11
00:00:23,215 --> 00:00:25,515
Water evaporates
from that edge,

12
00:00:25,545 --> 00:00:29,065
so water from the middle
flows out to replace it.

13
00:00:29,065 --> 00:00:31,505
The flow carries
the specks along.

14
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,855
As the drop thins,
the flow speeds up.

15
00:00:35,855 --> 00:00:38,170
In the last moments,
the specks pile up

16
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:39,155
at the edge.

17
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:42,325
Make the specks oval,
and the ring can vanish.

18
00:00:43,153 --> 00:00:45,143
Measure it:
in this model,

19
00:00:45,143 --> 00:00:47,563
nine in 10 specks
reach the ring.

20
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,655
Oval specks cling
together at the surface,

21
00:00:50,835 --> 00:00:52,945
which can hold them back
from the edge.

22
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:54,270
A stuck edge

23
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:57,500
and an outward flow:
that's all a ring takes.

24
00:00:58,187 --> 00:00:59,947
Next: listen carefully

25
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:01,922
to a mug filling
with water.

26
00:01:02,077 --> 00:01:03,266
You can hear it rise.
